---
import { isServicePath } from '../lib/service-routes';
import { API_DOCS_HREF, CHANGELOG_HREF, manualForPath } from '../lib/api-docs';
/**
 * THE PORTAL HEADER, laid out left to right: brand · divider · nav · search · preferences.
 *
 * Starlight's own header is a three-column grid whose first column is sized to the SIDEBAR's width
 * and carries `overflow: clip`, so the search box lines up with the content column no matter how
 * long the site title is. That is a good rule for a pure docs site and the wrong one here: this
 * portal has three destinations to name before anything is searched, and the nav has to sit beside
 * the brand rather than at the far right of the row (Oleh, 2026-08-19). Once the nav is in the left
 * group the grid's whole reason for existing is gone, so this is a flex row instead.
 *
 * WHAT WE GIVE UP by owning this file: the search box no longer aligns with the content column's
 * centre line, and any header change Starlight ships in a future version lands here by hand. That
 * is the cost of the layout being asked for, and it is worth stating out loud — this is the fifth
 * component we override and the one that owns the most.
 *
 * MOBILE: below 50rem Starlight hides the preferences group and shows the menu button, and this
 * file keeps that behaviour by keeping the same `sl-hidden md:sl-flex` classes. The nav is hidden
 * with it and re-declared as two top-level sidebar entries — the mobile menu renders the sidebar,
 * so that is where a phone reaches Roadmap and Contact.
 */
/* REAL PATHS, NOT `virtual:starlight/*`. Starlight's own components import each other through
   virtual modules, but those have no type declarations outside the package — `astro check` reports
   six `ts(2307)` on them, which is a gate failing on code that runs. So: our two overrides by
   relative path, and the three we do not override from the package. The trade is that a LATER
   override of ThemeSelect, LanguageSelect or SocialIcons through `components:` would not reach this
   file; whoever adds one has to swap the import here too. */
import Icon from './Icon.astro';
import Search from './Search.astro';
import SiteTitle from './SiteTitle.astro';
import LanguageSelect from '@astrojs/starlight/components/LanguageSelect.astro';
import SocialIcons from '@astrojs/starlight/components/SocialIcons.astro';
import SessionControl from './SessionControl.astro';

/* DOCUMENTATION DOES NOT POINT AT `/`, and that is the shape of this navigation. `/` is the WELCOME
   screen — the front door, reached by clicking the brand, with its own hero and its own search. It
   is not a page of the manual, so nothing in this list lights up while you stand on it.
   Documentation points at the manual's first page instead. */
/* `MY REQUESTS` IS A HEADER DESTINATION (Oleh, 2026-08-21), and the reason it was not is spent.
   `design.md` §2 argued it earned no slot "until sign-in exists — a fourth item leading to a locked
   page is a fourth item that fails for every visitor". The session now defaults to `in` and
   `/requests/` renders the list, so that sentence describes a page that no longer exists. It was
   living as a grey line at the foot of `/contact/`, which is where a destination goes to not be
   found.

   THE EVIDENCE SAYS THE SAME. Of fifteen customer portals swept on 2026-08-19, Zapier is the only
   one with a public ticket system, and it carries `Contact Support` and `My Requests` as two
   separate header items — because "open one" and "see the ones I opened" are different jobs at
   different frequencies. It is not a sixth tile on `/contact/`: that grid answers "what kind of
   thing are you about to write", and a destination is not a kind of writing. The grid is also a
   measured 3+2 over six tracks, and a sixth tile breaks it.

   `Documentation` BECAME `Docs` TO PAY FOR IT, and the number is why. Measured at 390: the nav row
   has 342px and today's three labels need 282, so nothing scrolls. A fourth item at the old labels
   needs 379 and would start a horizontal scroll on every phone. `Docs · Roadmap · Contact us · My
   requests` needs 312 and keeps 30px of room. One shortened word buys the whole destination, and
   `Docs` is what the same nav already says on `/handoff/`. */
/* `API/MCP` IS THE FIFTH ITEM. IT WAS `API`, THE ARITHMETIC BELOW IS WHY, AND DAN OVERRULED IT ON
   2026-08-24 HAVING SEEN THE SHIPPED VERSION: "the menu should say API MCP, I think MCP needs to be
   in there." The arithmetic was not wrong, it was outvoted, and it is kept because the cost is still
   being paid every day and the next person should know what it is rather than rediscover it.

   THE BUDGET. At 390 the nav row has 342px, the gap between pills is 0 and each pill carries 10px of
   padding on each side, so a pill costs its label plus 20. From the three measurements already
   recorded above: `Docs · Roadmap · Contact us · My requests` = 312px, and the same set with
   `Contact` instead of `Contact us` = 292px. Subtracting the padding, `My requests` is 77px of text
   over 11 glyphs — 7.0px a glyph — which is the rate everything below is derived at, with capitals
   taken 25% wider than that as they are in every sans of this proportion.

     `API`        ≈ 22px of text →  42px pill
     `API/MCP`    ≈ 57px of text →  77px pill
     `API & MCP`  ≈ 68px of text →  88px pill
     `Developers` ≈ 76px of text →  96px pill

   THE FOUR OPTIONS, EACH AGAINST 342:
     keep `Contact us`, add `API/MCP`   312 + 77 = 389   47px over — scrolls on every phone
     shorten to `Contact`, `API/MCP`    292 + 77 = 369   27px over — scrolls on every phone   ← now
     shorten to `Contact`, `API & MCP`  292 + 88 = 380   38px over
     shorten to `Contact`, `API`        292 + 42 = 334   8px spare — nothing scrolls

   WHAT IT ACTUALLY COSTS, MEASURED IN THE BROWSER AT 390 RATHER THAN ESTIMATED. Every number below
   is a reading of `nav.scrollWidth` against `nav.clientWidth`, taken on the day named:

     08-23  `API`, five items                     353 against 310 →   43px of scroll
     08-24  `API/MCP`, five items                 388 against 310 →   78px  (+35)
     08-24  `API · MCP`, five items               395 against 310 →   85px  (+42)
     08-25  `API/MCP` + `Changelog`, six items    480 against 310 →  170px  (+92)
     08-25  the same, `My requests` moved out     378 against 310 →   68px  (−102)   ← now

   THE ROW WAS ALREADY SCROLLING BEFORE ANY OF THIS: the 8px of spare the table above predicts did
   not survive contact with the real font, and every estimate here runs about 19px optimistic. Read
   the measurements, not the arithmetic; the arithmetic is kept because it is how a candidate label
   gets costed before anyone builds it.

   `Changelog` cost 92px, which is what took the strip past anything defensible. What paid for it was
   moving `My requests` out of this row and behind the account control in `SessionControl.astro` —
   a change that is not about width, and whose width argument is only the reason it happened now. It
   returned 102px, more than the changelog spent, so the row is narrower today than it was before
   either landed. The header and the page overflow at no width; only this strip scrolls, which the
   note in the styles below calls the honest failure mode.

   WHAT IT BUYS: a reader scanning the header for the letters M-C-P now finds them. The old answer
   was that the first thing behind the door names MCP — `/api/` says it in its lede, the sidebar
   there shows `API` and `MCP` as two groups, and the docs search indexes both — and Dan, who owns
   the product, judged that too far behind the door.

   `Contact us` → `Contact`. Dan overruled a label change on this item on 2026-08-20, and this is not
   a reversal of it: his ruling was about MEANING — the door must not be named after one of its four
   rooms, which is why "Report a problem" was wrong. `Contact` is the same neutral door with the
   pronoun dropped, and it is what the four-item measurement at 292px was taken on.

   ONE NUMBER HERE IS ESTIMATED, NOT MEASURED: the 42px `API` pill. The 292 and 312 figures were read
   off the page; the glyph rate they imply is what sizes the new pill. 8px of the 342 is the margin
   that estimate has to be wrong by, so this wants one confirmation at 390 before it is quoted as
   measured. */
/* `CHANGELOG` IS THE SIXTH ITEM, AND IT IS THE MOST EXPENSIVE ONE THIS ROW HAS TAKEN. Oleh,
   2026-08-25: it goes before `Contact`. Its position among the first three is the part that was
   open, and it is placed BEFORE `Roadmap` because the first three items are things to READ and the
   last three are things to DO — write to us, watch a plan, look up what you sent. Changelog and
   Roadmap are the same subject at two tenses (what shipped, what is planned) and belong next to
   each other; putting the shipped half on the reading side of the boundary keeps both groupings
   intact, which `Changelog · Roadmap` between the manuals and the contact door does.

   THE ARITHMETIC, computed exactly the way the `API/MCP` block above computes it — 7.0px a glyph
   from the measured `My requests`, capitals 25% wider, plus 20px of pill padding:

     `Changelog`  8 lowercase at 7.0 + one capital at 8.75 ≈ 65px of text → 85px pill

   Against the 342px the nav row has at 390: the five items are 369 (27px over, the line above), and
   six are 292 + 77 + 85 = 454. THAT IS 112px OVER, four times the overflow this row already carries,
   and it is the number to quote when somebody asks what the changelog cost.

   ESTIMATED, NOT MEASURED, and the gap is known to run one way. When `API/MCP` was measured in the
   browser at 390 it came out at 388px of nav against a 310px strip (78px of scroll) where this
   arithmetic predicted 369 against 342 (27px). Applying +85 to the measured figure rather than the
   estimated one projects roughly 473 against 310, about 163px of horizontal scroll. Both numbers are
   worth having: the first is the one this table is internally consistent with, the second is the one
   a phone will actually do. Neither has been read in a browser since this item was added.

   WHAT IS NOT PAID FOR. The row scrolls; the header and the page do not overflow at any width, which
   the note in the styles below calls the honest failure mode. There is no shortening left that is
   free: `Changelog` is the word for the thing, `Log` and `Updates` are both wronger and only
   `Updates` is shorter. The real fix is structural (a menu behind one item, or the nav moving into
   the mobile sheet below 50rem), and that is a decision about the header rather than about this
   item, so it is not taken here. It is recorded here so the next person inherits the number instead
   of rediscovering it. */
const NAV = [
  { label: 'Docs', href: '/start/what-baseout-is/' },
  { label: 'API/MCP', href: API_DOCS_HREF },
  { label: 'Changelog', href: CHANGELOG_HREF },
  { label: 'Roadmap', href: '/roadmap/' },
  { label: 'Contact', href: '/contact/' },
];

/* THE LABEL IS "CONTACT US" AND THE URL IS `/contact/`, which now agree. They did not for a while:
   the label read "Report a problem" on the argument that it named the lane almost everyone arrives
   for. Dan overruled it on 2026-08-20, and he was right for a reason the earlier argument missed.
   The page holds four lanes now, and three of them are not problems: a wish, a billing question and
   a catch-all. A door labelled after one of its four rooms tells three quarters of the people
   standing in front of it that they are in the wrong place. The step inside still names itself, so
   nothing was lost by making the door neutral. */
const here = Astro.url.pathname;

/* `Documentation` used to light up for anything that was not the roadmap or the contact form, which
   made it current on `/requests/` — a service page with no documentation in it. The docs entry is
   current only when the reader is on a docs page AND not on a service page.

   THE API MANUAL IS THE THIRD THING `Docs` MUST NOT CLAIM. `/api/` and `/mcp/` are content-collection
   pages rendered with the full docs chrome, so every test for "is this documentation" answers yes
   for them — which is exactly how `Docs` would end up lit while the reader stands in the other
   manual. It is subtracted here for the same reason a service path is, and from the same single
   source (`lib/api-docs.ts`) that `routeData.ts` partitions the sidebar with. */
/* THE CHANGELOG IS THE FOURTH THING `Docs` MUST NOT CLAIM, for the same reason and by the same
   mechanism: `/changelog/` and its entries are content-collection pages rendered with the full docs
   chrome. `manualForPath` names which manual a path is in and `'product'` is the remainder, so a new
   product chapter still lights `Docs` without being registered anywhere, while every manual added
   beside it subtracts itself. */
const manual = manualForPath(here);
const isDocs = here !== '/' && !isServicePath(here) && manual === 'product';

const isCurrent = (href: string) => {
  if (href === API_DOCS_HREF) return manual === 'api';
  if (href === CHANGELOG_HREF) return manual === 'changelog';
  if (href.startsWith('/roadmap') || href.startsWith('/contact') || href.startsWith('/requests'))
    return here.startsWith(href.replace(/\/$/, ''));
  return isDocs;
};
---

<div class="header">
  <div class="title-wrapper sl-flex">
    <SiteTitle />
  </div>

  {/* The same rule Starlight uses to fence off the theme selector, reused so the two dividers in
      this row are one idea rather than two lookalikes. It is a desktop-only ornament: on a phone the
      nav is on its own row and has nothing to be fenced from. */}
  <div class="hx-rule sl-hidden md:sl-flex" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  {/* NO `sl-hidden md:sl-flex` HERE ANY MORE. Starlight hides its whole right-hand group below
      50rem, and the nav used to go with it — which meant the two services had to be re-declared as
      sidebar entries so a phone could reach them, and THAT put them into the documentation's
      reading order (prev/next is derived from the sidebar). One nav that survives the breakpoint
      removes the duplicate and its side effect at once. Below 50rem it wraps to its own row; see
      the `--sl-nav-height` note in the styles. */}
  {/* THE ROW IS ASKED WHETHER IT FITS, AND IS GIVEN SOMEWHERE TO PUT WHAT DOES NOT (2026-08-25).
      This is the structural fix the `Changelog` block above says the row needs and declines to
      take: *"The real fix is structural (a menu behind one item, or the nav moving into the mobile
      sheet below 50rem)"*. It is the first of those two.

      The whole cost table above is now history rather than a budget. It priced labels at 7.0px a
      glyph to find out in advance whether a candidate would fit, and its own postscript records the
      estimate running ~19px optimistic; `lib/nav-overflow.ts` reads the pills the browser laid out
      instead, so a label change costs a measurement rather than an argument. Keep the table: it is
      the record of what each item cost and why `Contact us` became `Contact`, and a future reader
      asking "why is this word short" should find the answer rather than re-shorten something else.

      NOTHING IS HIDDEN AND NOTHING IS REORDERED. Every item is in the DOM at every width; folding
      moves an item from the row into the menu and back. The item carrying `aria-current` is never
      the one that folds — measured before this landed: on `/contact/` at 390 the current pill
      painted the letters `Con` at the viewport edge, which is the answer to "where am I" being the
      one thing cut off. */}
  <nav class="hx sl-flex" aria-label="Portal" data-nav-fit>
    {NAV.map((item) => (
      <a
        class="hx-link"
        href={item.href}
        data-nav-item
        aria-current={isCurrent(item.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
      >
        {item.label}
      </a>
    ))}

    {/* `<details>`, so the browser owns the toggle, the keyboard and the open state — the same
        reason `ChatDock.astro` draws its cited-sources list as one. Ships `hidden`: with no script
        the nav keeps the `overflow-x: auto` it shipped with, which is a scrolling row rather than a
        row with items missing. */}
    <details class="hx-more" data-nav-more hidden>
      <summary class="hx-link hx-more-btn" aria-label="More portal sections">
        More
        <span class="hx-more-caret" aria-hidden="true">
          <Icon name="chevron-down" size={14} />
        </span>
      </summary>
      <div class="hx-menu">
        {NAV.map((item) => (
          <a
            class="hx-menu-item"
            href={item.href}
            data-nav-copy
            hidden
            aria-current={isCurrent(item.href) ? 'page' : undefined}
          >
            {item.label}
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </details>
  </nav>

  {/* SEARCH SITS ON THE RIGHT, not between the nav and the preferences. Centred it was the widest
      thing on the row and read as the header's subject; the subject is the brand and where you are.
      Unconditional: Starlight only skips the search when Pagefind is off AND the slot is stock, and
      this portal overrides the slot — the pair is always ours to render. */}
  <div class="hx-search sl-flex print:hidden">
    <Search />
  </div>

  {/* The group survives the breakpoint too, but sheds everything except the theme control: with the
      docs sidebar gone from the service pages there is no mobile menu on them to hold it, and a
      theme switch that exists on some pages and not others is worse than a slightly busier row. */}
  {/* THE IDENTITY IS THE LAST THING IN THE ROW, which is where Dan asked for it and where a reader
      reaches for it. `ThemeSelect` is gone from here: it was a ~100px labelled select carrying a word
      most readers set once, and `ThemeToggle` does the same job in 32px. That difference is what
      pays for the identity control rather than pushing the row into a scroll on a phone. */}
  <div class="sl-flex print:hidden right-group">
    <div class="sl-hidden md:sl-flex social-icons">
      <SocialIcons />
    </div>
    <div class="sl-hidden md:sl-flex">
      <LanguageSelect />
    </div>
    <SessionControl />
  </div>
</div>

<script>
  import { wireNavOverflow } from '../lib/nav-overflow';
  wireNavOverflow();
</script>

<style>
  .header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 16px;
    height: 100%;
  }

  .title-wrapper {
    flex: none;
    /* Kept from Starlight: a long title must not grow into the row and push the search off it. */
    overflow: clip;
    padding: 0.25rem;
    margin: -0.25rem;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .hx-rule {
    flex: none;
    width: 1px;
    height: 24px;
    background: var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  /* `min-width: 0`, NOT `flex: none` (2026-08-26). Every other item in this row is `flex: none`, so
     the nav is the only shrinkable one and it absorbs the whole deficit when the row runs out —
     which is what makes `clientWidth` the room available rather than the nav's own content width,
     and therefore what lets `lib/nav-overflow.ts` see the overflow at all. Its header carries the
     measurement. The pills do not shrink with it: `white-space: nowrap` holds each one's min-content
     at its label, so the widths the fitter reads stay true while the box tells it the budget. */
  .hx {
    min-width: 0;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 2px;
  }

  .hx-link {
    padding: 6px 10px;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-6);
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    font-weight: 500;
    text-decoration: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
    transition: background-color 140ms ease, color 140ms ease;
  }

  .hx-link:hover {
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    color: var(--sl-color-white);
  }

  /* The current section is stated, not implied. A header that never says where you are is a list of
     places you could go and no answer to the question you actually have. */
  .hx-link[aria-current='page'] {
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
    color: var(--bo-soft-primary-fg);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .hx-link:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .hx-link[hidden] { display: none; }

  /* ── THE OVERFLOW MENU ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Wears `.hx-link` so the trigger is the same pill as the items beside it: a control that folds a
     row of pills and does not look like one reads as a different kind of thing, and a reader has to
     learn it. The caret is the only difference, and it is the standard one. */
  .hx-more {
    position: relative;
    flex: none;
  }

  .hx-more[hidden] { display: none; }

  .hx-more-btn {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 4px;
    cursor: pointer;
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Safari draws its own disclosure triangle through a pseudo-element `list-style` cannot reach. */
  .hx-more-btn::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }

  .hx-more-caret {
    display: flex;
    transition: transform 140ms ease;
  }

  .hx-more[open] > .hx-more-btn > .hx-more-caret { transform: rotate(180deg); }

  /* THE TRIGGER SPEAKS FOR THE ITEM IT IS HOLDING. `nav-overflow.ts` folds the current item only
     when the row cannot hold it at all, and when that happens the row would otherwise answer "where
     am I" with nothing. Same soft-teal pair as `aria-current` itself, so it is the same statement
     rather than a second one to learn. */
  .hx-more[data-holds-current] > .hx-more-btn {
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
    color: var(--bo-soft-primary-fg);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .hx-menu {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-start: calc(100% + 4px);
    inset-inline-end: 0;
    z-index: 40;
    min-width: 168px;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    padding: 4px;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: var(--bo-base-100);
    /* The popover rung of the elevation vocabulary (DESIGN.md §4): menus float above the page
       rather than sit in it, and this is the one of the three shadows that says so. */
    box-shadow: 0 12px 32px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.24);
  }

  .hx-menu-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 10px 12px;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-6);
    color: var(--bo-muted-strong);
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    font-weight: 500;
    text-decoration: none;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .hx-menu-item[hidden] { display: none; }

  .hx-menu-item:hover {
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    color: var(--sl-color-white);
  }

  .hx-menu-item[aria-current='page'] {
    background: var(--bo-soft-primary-bg);
    color: var(--bo-soft-primary-fg);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .hx-menu-item:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  /* The search pair has its own natural width (a field plus the ask button); letting it grow would
     make the one control on this row that is already the widest wider still. `auto` here is what
     pushes it and everything after it to the far end, so the empty space lands between the nav and
     the search rather than after it. */
  .hx-search {
    flex: none;
    margin-inline-start: auto;
  }

  .right-group {
    flex: none;
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .social-icons {
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .social-icons::after {
    content: '';
    height: 2rem;
    border-inline-end: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  /* ── BELOW 50rem THE HEADER IS A GRID OF TWO ROWS ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     Row one is the mark, the search and the theme control; row two is the nav, across the whole
     width. The nav got the full row after the first attempt left it 198px and cut a label in half. The nav used to
     vanish at this width, which is why the two services had to be duplicated as sidebar entries —
     and that duplication is what put them into the documentation's prev/next sequence. One nav that
     survives the breakpoint removes both.

     GRID, NOT `flex-wrap`. Wrapping was tried and it breaks wherever the width happens to run out:
     measured at 390 that put the theme control on a row of its own and the nav on a third, three
     rows deep inside a bar sized for two. An explicit row break helped and then consumed a line of
     its own. Named areas say where each thing goes and the arithmetic stops mattering.

     `--sl-nav-height` is Starlight's own variable and everything downstream is computed from it: the
     fixed header's height, `.main-frame`'s top padding, and the sidebar pane's `inset-block`.
     Raising it is the documented way to make the bar taller; setting a height on the bar alone
     would slide the whole page underneath it. */
  /* ── THE BOUNDARY IS 700, NOT 50rem (Oleh, 2026-08-26: "не подобається, як тут виглядає хедер") ─
     He was looking at a 788px window, and the header there was the PHONE header: 104px tall, the
     wordmark gone, and **526px of empty gap** between a lone 28px brand mark and the search pair.
     Twelve pixels wider, at 800, it became a 64px single row with the wordmark back. A header that
     doubles in height and drops the brand name on a window most people would call a small laptop.

     THE 50rem WAS BORROWED, NOT MEASURED. It is Starlight's `md` — the width where IT collapses the
     docs sidebar and shows the menu button — and that is a real boundary for the sidebar and no
     boundary at all for this row. The two-row grid's own argument was measured at 390 ("the nav
     column was left 198px of a 358px row and cut a label in half"), which is true at 390 and false
     by a wide margin at 788.

     SO IT IS MEASURED. Swept on `/changelog/` with the wordmark hidden, reading how many of the five
     nav items survive on one line:

                       cursor    touch
                560     2 of 5    2 of 5
                600     3 of 5    3 of 5
                700     5 of 5    3 of 5
                720     5 of 5    3 of 5
                730     5 of 5    5 of 5   ← one row holds the whole nav on both

     Below it one row costs nav items while two rows show all five, so two rows are worth their 40px
     there. From it they are worth nothing: the same five items fit on one line and the bar is 56px
     instead of 104. `specs/16-responsive.md` §1 condition 2 — a measured min-content, with the
     measurement beside it.

     ONE NUMBER FOR BOTH POINTERS, and it is the touch one. A coarse pointer raises the search, ask
     and menu controls to the 44px floor, which costs the row about 30px, so the cursor crossover is
     700 and the touch crossover is 730. Splitting them would put two numbers on one idea and make
     the header's shape depend on how you are holding the device rather than on how wide it is. The
     cost of taking the larger is a 30px band where a cursor gets two rows it could have done
     without; the cost of taking the smaller is a touch user losing two nav items.

     THE MENU BUTTON IS NOT AFFECTED and must not be. It appears below Starlight's 50rem because
     that is when the docs sidebar goes away, which is its own question; the header simply reserves
     the gutter for it. Between 730 and 800 the row is one line AND carries a hamburger, and that is
     correct — verified `header.scrollWidth - clientWidth = 0` at 730 · 750 · 788 on both pointers. */
  @media (max-width: 729.98px) {
    :global(:root) { --sl-nav-height: 6.5rem; }

    .header {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: auto 1fr auto auto;
      grid-template-areas:
        'brand gap search prefs'
        'nav nav nav nav';
      align-items: center;
      row-gap: 8px;
      column-gap: 12px;
    }

    .title-wrapper { grid-area: brand; }
    .hx-search { grid-area: search; margin-inline-start: 0; }
    .right-group { grid-area: prefs; }

    .hx {
      grid-area: nav;
      min-width: 0;
      /* Measured at 390 after reclaiming the menu-button gutter: 343px of labels in a 342px row.
         The 2px gaps are the whole deficit — the pills carry 10px of padding each, so removing the
         gap leaves 20px between labels and nothing scrolls. */
      gap: 0;
      /* THE NO-SCRIPT FALLBACK, AND ONLY THAT (2026-08-25). This used to be the shipped behaviour
         and its comment called a hidden scroll "the honest failure mode: everything stays readable
         and reachable, and nothing is silently cut". Measured at 390 on fifteen routes, it was
         none of those things: 378px of items in 310px of row, `scrollbar-width: none` so no part of
         the screen says the row scrolls, and on `/contact/` the pill carrying `aria-current` painted
         the letters `Con` against the viewport edge. `lib/nav-overflow.ts` folds the excess into a
         menu instead and stamps `data-fit` when it has; this stays for the reader whose script
         never ran, where a scrolling row beats a truncated one. */
      overflow-x: auto;
      scrollbar-width: none;
    }

    .hx::-webkit-scrollbar { display: none; }

    /* Claimed by the fitter. A clipping box cannot render a menu that hangs out of it, and there is
       nothing left to scroll once every item either fits or has folded.

       The gap stays 0. It is a measured value (the pills carry 10px of padding each, so removing the
       gap leaves 20px between labels) and it is also an INPUT to the fitter, which reads `columnGap`
       off this element while every item is shown. Changing it here would silently change the
       arithmetic that decided what fits. */
    .hx[data-fit] { overflow: visible; }

    /* Starlight centres its menu button across the whole nav height, which is now two rows — it
       would float between them. Pin it to the first. */
    :global(starlight-menu-button button) {
      top: calc((3.25rem - var(--sl-menu-button-size)) / 2);
    }
  }
</style>
